The classic closure example — private state without a class:

```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function () {
    count += 1;
    return count;
  };
}

const counter = makeCounter();
counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2
counter(); // 3

const counter2 = makeCounter();
counter2(); // 1 — a fresh, independent count
```

`count` is not global, not a class field, not accessible from outside — the only way to touch it is through the function `makeCounter` returned. Each call to `makeCounter()` creates a brand-new `count` variable and a brand-new closure over it, which is why `counter` and `counter2` don't interfere with each other. This is the closure doing, with one function and one variable, what a class with a private field and one public method does with far more ceremony.

The single most common closures interview question, in JavaScript form:

```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// prints: 3, 3, 3 — not 0, 1, 2
```

`var` is **function-scoped**, not block-scoped — there is exactly *one* `i` for the whole loop, and all three arrow functions close over that same single variable. By the time any of the `setTimeout` callbacks actually runs, the loop has already finished and `i` is `3`.

The fix is `let` instead of `var`: `let` is **block-scoped**, so the loop creates a **fresh binding of `i` for every iteration**, and each closure captures its own separate variable.

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// prints: 0, 1, 2
```

Before `let` existed, the workaround was wrapping the loop body in an immediately-invoked function expression to manufacture a new scope per iteration by hand — exactly what `let` now does automatically.
